              {
                "text": "The rule: output is input horizontally concatenated with its vertical mirror (row reversed). Actually vmirror mirrors along vertical axis, i.e., reverses each row. Then hconcat(I, vmirror(I)) gives each row = row + reversed row, width doubled, height same.\n\n\"two times the row, but same height\" \u2014 width doubled. So output row = input row followed by reversed input row.\n\nLet me create a few demonstration inputs. Use various shapes/colors. Only orange? The rule says \"the orange grid only\" (color 7). But I can use any colors; verifier just does the transform. Better to give varied examples to help student infer.\n\nLet me show multiple inputs.\n\nExample 1: 3x3 as given maybe repeat? Better diverse.\n\nLet's do:\n- 2x2 with 7s\n- 1x4\n- 3x1\n- 4x4\n- rows not symmetric.\n\nLet me submit several.\n\nGrid 1:\n[[7,0],[0,7]] -> [[7,0,0,7],[0,7,7,0]]\n\nGrid 2:\n[[0,7,0,0]] -> [[0,7,0,0,0,0,7,0]]\n\nGrid 3:\n[[7],[0],[7]] -> [[7,7],[0,0],[7,7]]\n\nGrid 4 (larger, non-symmetric):\n[[7,0,0,7],[0,0,7,0],[7,7,0,0]] -> each row doubled mirrored.\n\nGrid 5 with colors? Rule mentions orange only, but using other colors could confuse. Keep to 7 and 0. Maybe include an example with mixed colors to show color-preserving? The task says orange grid only, so keep 7/0. But maybe variety helps. I'll stick to 7 and 0 mostly, maybe one with other color to show generality... Actually the Student needs to infer mirror; colors preserved. Using diverse colors is fine and probably helps. But rule says only orange visible. I'll use 7 and 0 to be safe, plus maybe one varied.\n\nI'll do 4-5 examples.\n\nLet me call in parallel multiple show_transformed_input.",
